 designed to handle situations where the error terms exhibit autocorrelation or heteroscedasticity. In such cases, the OLS estimator may no longer be the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator (BLUE). GLS modifies the estimation process to account for these issues, thereby improving the efficiency of the parameter estimates.\n\nThe GLS estimator can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\hat{\\beta}_{GLS} = (X' \\Omega^{-1} X)^{-1} X' \\Omega^{-1} y\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\Omega \\) is the covariance matrix of the error terms, which captures the structure of autocorrelation and/or heteroscedasticity.\n\nThe assumptions underlying GLS include:\n\n1. **Linearity**: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables remains linear.\n2. **Correct specification of the error structure**: The covariance matrix \\( \\Omega \\) must be correctly specified to reflect the nature of the error terms.\n3. **No perfect multicollinearity**: Similar to OLS, the independent variables should not be perfectly correlated.\n\nGLS is particularly useful in time series data or panel data where autocorrelation is common. By accounting for the correlation and varying variances in the error terms, GLS provides more reliable estimates than OLS in these contexts.\n\n**Relationship Between OLS and GLS**\n\nOLS can be viewed as a special case of GLS, applicable when the error terms are assumed to be homoscedastic and uncorrelated. In scenarios where these assumptions do not hold, GLS is preferred as it yields more efficient estimates. The choice between OLS and GLS ultimately depends on the underlying characteristics of the data and the validity of the assumptions.\n\nIn summary, while OLS is a widely used method for parameter estimation in regression analysis, GLS offers a robust alternative when faced with issues of autocorrelation and heteroscedasticity, ensuring that the estimates remain efficient and reliable. Understanding the strengths and limitations of both methods is crucial for effective econome